I woke in the dark. Not the gradual surfacing of ordinary sleep   the slow, comfortable drift from dreams to dawn that normal bodies perform without comment. This was a jolt. A full-system restart. My eyes opened to the ceiling of the Broken Antler and my body was already talking, every nerve ending filing its overnight report simultaneously, and the reports were not in agreement with the body I'd gone to sleep in.

The tingling was different today. Yesterday it had been systemic   a full-body hum, omnidirectional, the compound announcing itself across every inch of skin like a bell rung in an empty cathedral. Today it was focused. Concentrated. Three zones of intense, buzzing activit andthe chest, the hips, and   strangely, specifically   the lips.

My lips. I touched them in the dark, fingertips finding the shape by feel. The lower lip was   fuller. Not subtly. Not the marginal, deniable plumpness of yesterday's inventory, the kind of change you could attribute to dry air or dehydration or the general unreliability of a man examining his own face at five in the morning. This was measurable. The lower lip sat differently against the upper   more cushion, more give, a softness that my fingertips mapped with horrified precision. The surface was smoother. Tender. When I pressed lightly, the sensation was vivid   not painful, but intensely present, as if each cell in the lip tissue had been replaced by a cell with twice the nerve density.

I ran my finger across the upper lip. The stubble that should have been there   the overnight shadow, the persistent sandpaper rasp that greeted my fingers every morning of my adult life   was a ghost. A memory. I pressed harder, as if pressure could summon the hair back from wherever it had gone. Nothing. Smooth skin. Not shaved-smooth   the freshly-razored feeling had a different texture, a different resistance. This was skin that had simply stopped producing facial hair in this region. The follicles had retired. Packed their microscopic bags and emigrated without notice.

The chin still had something. A faint stubble, thin and sparse, concentrated on the point of the chin and the lower jaw. But even there, it was retreating. Yesterday's patchy shadow had become today's suggestion of a shadow   visible only if you knew to look for it, which I did, because I'd been wearing that shadow for twelve years and its absence was louder than any sound.

[[Check the mirror|day_5_1_1]]

:: day_5_1_1

Mira was asleep beside me. Behind me, technically   her arm draped across my waist, her knees tucked behind mine, the spooning position that had become our default sleeping arrangement somewhere around Kestwick. Her breathing was slow and deep and steady, the half-elf sleep rhythm that extracted maximum restoration from minimum time. I could feel her heartbeat against my back   slow, strong, metronomic. Her arm across my waist felt different than it had even yesterday. Not because her arm had changed. Because the waist it rested on had.

I needed to see. Needed the mirror, even in the predawn dark, even knowing that the cheap polished tin would show me things I wasn't ready to catalog. I lifted Mira's arm   carefully, slowly, the practiced motion of a man who'd learned through painful experience that waking Mira unexpectedly could result in a reflexive elbow strike that left bruises   and slid out from under the blanket.

The air hit my skin. Every inch of it. Not cold   the room was mild, the autumn not yet deep enough to freeze   but present. Textured. The air itself had a quality that my skin read like braille, every current and temperature variation rendered in high definition by nerve endings that had been calibrated overnight to receive information at frequencies I'd never registered before. The fine hairs on my forearms   the few that remained   stood at attention. Goosebumps rose across my chest, my stomach, my thighs. Not from cold. From sensitivity. From a body that was receiving the world at a resolution it hadn't asked for and couldn't turn down.

I crossed the room. Three steps. The floorboards were a map under my bare feet   each plank a different temperature, a different grain, each nail head a cold dot against the sole. I stood in front of the polished tin mirror and leaned close.

/* <img src="img/scene/day_5/1.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" /> */
Predawn light. Gray. Thin. Merciless in the way that only pre-sunrise illumination could be   too honest, too flat, stripping away the forgiving warmth that afternoon and candlelight would offer. The mirror was still cheap and still warped and still showed me an approximation rather than a truth. But the approximation was sufficient to deliver the verdict.

My face. Still mine. Still recognizable   the green eyes, deep-set, hooded, looking back at me from beneath dark brows with the particular expression of a man examining evidence he doesn't want to admit to. The scar through the left eyebrow, thin and pale and stubbornly present. The crooked half-smile was there too, though I wasn't smiling   the natural asymmetry of my mouth, the slight upturn at the right corner, unchanged.

But the frame around those landmarks had shifted overnight. The jaw was softer. Not soft   not feminine, not rounded   but the hard, squared corners that had defined the lower geometry of my face since adolescence were easing. Rounding. The angles filing themselves down into something less blunt, less aggressive, less immediately and unambiguously male. If I clenched my teeth, the jaw muscles still popped at the hinges   still there, still functional. But the bone beneath them was migrating. Slowly. Microscopically. A fraction of a millimeter at a time, the architecture of my lower face was being redrawn by an invisible hand.

[[Pull up the undershirt|day_5_1_2]]

:: day_5_1_2

The skin. Gods, the skin. Two days ago, the softening had been subtle   a texture change, a smoothness that could be attributed to a good wash or Ysolde's healing compounds. Yesterday, it had been visible but deniable. Today, it was neither. My face glowed in the gray light. Actually glowed   not with light, but with a quality of surface that caught and held the faint illumination in a way weathered skin simply didn't. The pores were tighter. The texture was finer. The overall effect was of a man who'd spent a month in a bathhouse being scrubbed and oiled and tended by professionals, emerging with skin that belonged to someone ten years younger and considerably more pampered. I pulled my undershirt up.

/* <img src="img/scene/day_5/2.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" /> */
The chest. The chest was where the compound had been working hardest overnight, and the chest was where the evidence was most damning. The body hair was   not thinning anymore. Gone. The thick dark mat that had covered my pectorals from collarbone to sternum since I was fifteen had been reduced to scattered wisps, fine and pale, clinging to skin that gleamed in the mirror like polished stone. I could count the remaining strands. Fourteen on the left pectoral. Eleven on the right. The trail of dark hair down my midline   the line that connected chest to navel to groin, the central meridian of masculine topography   was a shadow of itself, fine and sparse, visible only because I knew where to look.

The nipples. I didn't want to look at the nipples. I looked at the nipples.

They were larger. The areolae had expanded again overnight   wider, darker, the color deepening from rosy pink to something approaching a warm mauve, the contrast against the lighter, smoother skin making them more prominent, more visible, more undeniably different. The nipples themselves protruded slightly from their centers   small raised nubs, tender and tight in the cool air, standing at attention with the automatic, involuntary response that I'd learned to associate with the new sensitivity. They were no longer the flat, inconsequential discs I'd owned for twenty-six years. They were   present. Dimensional. Objects with height and width and a nerve density that announced itself to every fabric, every breeze, every accidental contact with devastating clarity.

I didn't touch them. I'd learned that lesson.

Instead, I looked at the pectorals themselves. Still muscle. Still thick, still hard, still the flat slabs of dense tissue that ten years of swordwork had deposited on my chest frame. But the shape was   I turned to the side. Profile. In the gray light, from this angle, the edge of the pectoral was not quite flat. Not quite the clean, straight line from collarbone to sternum that defined a man's chest. There was a curve. Barely. A suggestion of volume at the lower edge of the muscle, a faint convexity where concavity should have been, the tissue behind the nipple creating the shallowest possible arc between the nipple and the muscle beneath.

Two millimeters, Nella had said. Maybe three millimeters now. Not visible through clothing. Not visible to anyone but me, standing shirtless in a gray room, mapping my own chest with the obsessive attention of a man watching his architecture be remodeled from the inside.

But I could feel it. When I pressed my palm flat against the left pectoral   gently, carefully, avoiding the incendiary nipple   there was give. Beneath the muscle. Between the muscle and the ribs. A thin layer of tissue that yielded under pressure in a way muscle didn't, a softness that had no business existing on a swordsman's chest and was establishing itself regardless, patient and purposeful and completely indifferent to my opinion on the matter.

[[Pull down the smallclothes|day_5_1_3]]

:: day_5_1_3

I dropped the undershirt. Pushed my smallclothes down. The waist.

/* <img src="img/scene/day_5/3.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" /> */
The belt test. I pressed my hands against both sides, the daily measurement that had become ritual, the width check that my body performed now with the automatic anxiety of a merchant counting his coin. Thumbs on the hipbones, fingers pressing inward, mapping the distance between the two sides of my torso at the narrowest point.

Narrower. Again. Not by an inch   by a fraction, a sliver, the kind of change that a cloth measuring tape would catch and a casual glance would miss. But I caught it. My hands were calibrated to this body's dimensions by twenty-six years of residence, and the readings they returned were wrong. The waist was pulling in. Cinching. The oblique muscles and whatever sat beneath them were being drawn inward on a slow, invisible winch, tightening the circumference of my midsection while the bones of my hips began their lateral migration outward.

The hips ached. A deep, persistent, bone-level throb that lived beneath the skin and beneath the muscle and beneath everything that could be massaged or medicated or ignored. This was skeletal. The bones themselves, the massive plates of the pelvis, were being told to widen, and they were complying with the glacial obedience of geological processes   too slow to watch, too persistent to stop.

I turned. Looked at myself from the side.

The silhouette was wrong. Not dramatically. Not in a way that would register through clothing, not in a way that would make a stranger on the street do a double-take. But my silhouette   the outline of my body in profile   had a curve it hadn't had four days ago. The waist-to-hip ratio was shifting. The straight, column-like torso of a male body was developing the faintest suggestion of an indentation at the waist and a corresponding flare at the hip, a taper that was millimeters from invisible and miles from acceptable.

My cock hung between my thighs. The same. Unchanged. I cupped it   the reflexive, confirmatory gesture   and the weight was right, the shape was right, the warm familiar heft of it exactly what it should be. Unchanged. Present. The one territory the compound hadn't breached.

But my hand against it felt different. The fingers that wrapped around the shaft were smoother at the tips, the calluses softer, the grip less rough against sensitive skin and the sensitivity itself was   amplified. Not in kind, but in degree. The casual touch that had produced a background hum of sensation last week now produced a foreground chord, richer, more detailed, more information per contact. I could feel the texture of my own palm against the shaft, the grain of my own fingerprints, the specific topography of skin on skin rendered in a resolution that was simultaneously fascinating and terrifying.

I let go. Pulled the smallclothes up. Looked at my forearms.

The hair was retreating. Not gone   still there, still visible, dark strands against skin that was lighter and smoother than it had been. But the density had halved. The thick forest of forearm hair that had been my baseline since adolescence was becoming a sparse grove, the individual strands finer, softer, less wire-like. The veins underneath were more visible   blue-green traces under skin that was losing its camouflage layer by layer.

My hands. I turned them over. The calluses   I pressed my right thumb into the pad of my left palm, into the thick ridge of keratin that swordwork had built over a decade of grip and swing and parry. The ridge was lower. Softer at the edges. The hard currency of my profession, being spent by a body that was liquidating assets I hadn't authorized for sale. I could still grip. The strength was there. But the interface between my hand and the world was thinning, the protective layer being sanded down by whatever cellular machinery was running inside me.

I looked at the mirror one final time. The whole picture. The body in the warped tin   still tall, still broad in the shoulders, still muscled, still recognizably the body of a man who fought for a living. But softer. Smoother. The surface being polished while the structure beneath began its slow, patient migration toward something else.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
We're leaving today and this body is leaving with me.
<</dialog>>

I said it to the mirror. To the horse-shaped water stain on the ceiling. To whatever version of myself would look back from whatever mirror I found on the road tomorrow, and the day after that, and the twenty-seven days after that.

[[Get dressed|day_5_2]]

:: day_5_2

The new tunic. The dark gray linen tent that Mira had bought yesterday from the market stall. I'd resented it when she purchased it. Hated the formlessness of it, the deliberate shapelessness, the way it hung from the shoulders like a sack rather than following the contours of the body beneath. It was a tunic designed to hide, and wearing it felt like admitting there was something to hide.

But when I pulled it over my head and the fabric settled against my skin, something complicated happened. The linen was softer than wool   drastically, dramatically softer. Where the rough weave of my old tunic had been a sustained assault on the rewired nipples, a constant low-level scream of friction and stimulation, the linen was   quiet. Gentle. The fabric draped rather than scratched, settled rather than abraded, and the change was so immediate and so profound that my breath left me in a shudder of relief I hadn't known I was waiting for.

The nipples still registered the fabric. Still felt it   the weight of the linen, the texture of the weave, the slight friction as I moved. But the volume had dropped from a shout to a murmur. The sustained, maddening chafing that had plagued me through yesterday's wool tunic was replaced by a whisper of contact that was   manageable. Almost pleasant. Almost.

I caught myself. The relief was the dangerous part. The relief meant I was adapting. Adjusting. Finding solutions to problems that shouldn't exist in the first place, and each solution was a step further from the body I was trying to get back to and a step closer to the body the compound was building without my consent.

The tunic was loose through the chest. Good. The faint convexity of the tissue behind the nipples was invisible under the generous cut, the dark color absorbing shadows that might otherwise betray the changed topography. Good. The waist   the tunic hung straight from the shoulders, the excess fabric concealing the narrowing that my hands could map but that no eye could detect through this much coverage.

My old trousers still fit. The hips were tighter   a persistent pinch at the sides where the seams pressed against bone that had widened by millimeters   but not unwearably so. I'd need new trousers eventually. Not today. Today, I could pass. Today, the disguise held.

[[Check the belt|day_5_2_1]]

:: day_5_2_1

The belt buckled one notch tighter than my baseline. One notch. A small adjustment on a leather strap, the kind of change that anyone might make after a few days of light eating or hard walking. Nothing remarkable. Nothing that would draw a comment. One notch.

Except I hadn't been eating lightly. I'd been eating ravenously, the compound driving a metabolic hunger that had me finishing every bowl of the Broken Antler's terrible stew and reaching for bread besides. The calories were going somewhere. Building something. Fueling the construction project that my body was running twenty-four hours a day without an architect I'd hired or blueprints I'd approved.

Boots. The boots still fit. The ankle that had been a ruined, swollen mess four days ago bore my weight without complaint, the miraculous healing a bitter reminder that the compound's first gift had been a genuine one   functional, life-saving, real   before its true purpose revealed itself. My calf wound was a closed pink line, healing clean, the twelve neat sutures Ysolde had placed dissolving into tissue that was replacing itself at a rate that would have taken a temple healer weeks to achieve.

I strapped on my sword belt. The longsword hung at my left hip, the familiar weight a comfort, the leather and steel of it unchanged by whatever was changing me. My sword didn't care about my skin texture or my nipple sensitivity or the softening calluses on the hands that gripped it. My sword cared about being drawn and swung and put away clean, and those were transactions my body could still perform.

I looked in the mirror one last time. Dark gray tunic, belted. Trousers tucked into boots. Sword at the hip. Male. Tall. Broad-shouldered. A mercenary heading out for a job. Nothing to see.

Nothing visible, anyway.

[[Check on Mira|day_5_2_2]]

:: day_5_2_2

Mira stirred behind me. The soldier's wake   asleep to alert between breaths, amber eyes open and focused before the lids had finished rising. She looked at me standing in the gray light, dressed and armed, and her expression performed the rapid assessment that was her version of a morning greeting   scanning my silhouette, checking the disguise, evaluating whether the man standing before her would pass inspection at the city gate.

<<dialog "Mira">>
The new tunic works. I can't see anything through it.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Good morning to you too.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
It is a morning. Whether it's good remains to be determined by the road, the weather, and the emotional stability of a Consortium surveyor I've never met. How's the chest?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Worse. The tissue is thicker. Three millimeters, maybe and the nipples are   larger. The areolae have expanded.
<</dialog>>

She sat up. The blanket fell to her waist and she was bare above it   the lean, tawny torso, the small firm breasts, the dark nipples that stood in sharp contrast to the half-elf's golden-brown skin. She looked at her own chest, then at mine   hidden under the gray linen   and something moved across her face that I couldn't read. Comparison? Recognition? The preemptive grief of a woman who could project the trajectory and see where it ended?

<<dialog "Mira">>
The linen helps?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Dramatically. The wool was   it was like being shouted at constantly. The linen whispers.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Good. I bought two. The second one's in the supply bag. Rotate them   wash one while you wear the other. Wet linen is worse than dry wool, so keep the spare dry.
<</dialog>>

She got up. Dressed with her usual efficiency   vest, linen shirt, trousers, boots, belt, bow. Each piece of gear snapping into place with the practiced automation of a woman who could arm herself in her sleep and probably had. She crossed the room, took my jaw in one hand, and turned my face to the light.

<<dialog "Mira">>
The stubble's almost gone. The jawline is   slightly different. Softer at the corners.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I know.
<</dialog>>

[[Check the facial hair|day_5_2_3]]

:: day_5_2_3

<<dialog "Mira">>
Can you grow any facial hair at all? Even just the chin?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
The chin still has something. Barely. Give it another day and I doubt even that survives.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
We'll say the healing compound affects hair growth. A known side effect. Temporary. The skin improvement and the hair reduction are both attributable to the same cover story. Keep it simple.
<</dialog>>

She released my jaw. Her thumb swept once across my lower lip   a gesture so quick I almost missed it, the pad of her thumb dragging across the fuller, softer surface and registering the change before she pulled her hand away and turned to the supply bags.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Your lips are different.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I noticed.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Fuller. Pinker. The color is deeper. You look like you've been kissing someone for an hour.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Mira  
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I'm observing. That's what you asked me to do. Observe and report. Repor andyour lips are becoming very pretty. Observation complete. Let's go meet our surveyor.
<</dialog>>

She shouldered her pack and headed for the door. I grabbed the supply bag, the heavier one, and followed. My hips ached with the bone-deep throb as I took the stairs, each step a reminder that beneath the trousers and the boots and the belt buckled one notch tighter, the scaffolding of my body was being redesigned.

<<addobjective "day_5" "Meet Hadden Marsh at the east gate">>
[[Meet Hadden Marsh|day_5_3]]

:: day_5_3

The common room was nearly empty at this hour   a scattering of dock workers nursing pre-shift ales and the innkeeper performing his eternal vigil behind the ledger, his pen scratching like a mechanical insect. Tess was wiping tables with a rag that was distributing grime more than removing it. She glanced up as we passed.

<<dialog "Tess">>
Heading out? I'll hold your room for three days, like she asked. After that, it's back on the market.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Three days will be fine. Thanks, Tess.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Tess">>
Safe travels. Whatever you're using for that skin, keep using it. You look five years younger.
<</dialog>>

I didn't look back. Mira's hand found the small of my back and steered me through the door with the firm, redirecting pressure of a woman who could hear the conversation going somewhere unhelpful and was choosing to end it through physical navigation.

The morning air of Valdmere hit us at the threshold. Cold. Damp. The autumn chill that lived in this city like a permanent resident, paying rent in condensation and collecting interest in rheumatism. My skin   my new, treacherously sensitive skin   registered every particle of it. The temperature. The humidity. The faint chemical tang of the harbor carried on a breeze from the south. The distant smell of baking bread from the bakery on Mill Street, three blocks away, rendered in olfactory detail that would have been invisible to me a week ago.
<<set $day_5_3_1 = false>>
<<addobjective "day_5" "Walk east to meet Hadden Marsh at the east gate">>
<<link "Walk east to meet Hadden Marsh at the east gate">>
    <<regionmap "valdmere">>
<</link>>

:: day_5_3_1
<<set $day_5_3_1 = true>>
We walked east through the waking city. The streets were coming alive   merchants opening shutters, cart horses clopping on cobblestones, a woman dumping a chamber pot from a second-story window with a warning shout that came three seconds too late for the unfortunate man below. Ordinary morning. Ordinary city. The world functioning exactly as it had yesterday and the day before and every day since Valdmere was built, oblivious to the fact that one of its temporary residents was being disassembled and rebuilt by a vial of green-black liquid he'd drunk in a cellar.

The east gate was large   two heavy wooden doors bound in iron, flanked by stone towers that had been built for defense and were currently being used for pigeons. The guards here were marginally more alert than the ones at the south gate, though "marginally" was doing a lot of work in that sentence. They wore the city's colors   blue and gray, Valdmere's official palette of institutional depression   and carried halberds that looked like they hadn't been sharpened since the previous administration.

Standing in front of the gate, next to a mule that was eyeing the world with the particular contempt that only mules could achieve, was a man who could only be Hadden Marsh.

/* <img src="img/scene/day_5/4.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" /> */
He was exactly what Voss had described. Thin. Fussy. Wearing clothes that were too clean for travel   a pressed linen shirt under a buttoned waistcoat, trousers with actual creases, and boots that had been polished within the last hour and would lose that polish within the first mile. He wore spectacles   round, wire-framed, perched on a nose that was narrow and long and slightly reddened at the tip, suggesting either a cold or a constitutional tendency toward being cold. His hair was brown, receding, combed flat against his skull with something that had a faint herbal smell. He was perhaps forty-five, though he had the prematurely aged look of a man who spent his life indoors worrying about things that only he considered important.

His leather satchel was enormous   bulging with notebooks, charcoal sticks, measuring tools, folded maps, and what appeared to be a small magnifying glass on a chain. The mule beside him   a gray, sturdy, extremely displeased animal   bore two large packs of surveying equipment and camping supplies. The mule looked at us with the expression of a creature that had been hired for this job against its will and was planning to file a formal complaint.

[[Talk to Hadden|day_5_3_2]]

:: day_5_3_2

<<dialog "Hadden">>
You're the escort? Both of you?
<</dialog>>

His voice was precise. Clipped. Each word spoken with the enunciation of a man who treated language as a technical instrument and expected it to perform to specification.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Both of us. I shoot things from far away. He hits things up close. Between us, you'll survive.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Hadden">>
Encouraging. I was told you were professionals.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
We are professionals. Professional survival is our primary service offering.
<</dialog>>

Hadden looked at me. His gaze was the same kind of assessment that Voss had performed   face, body, weapons   but where Voss's evaluation was financial, Hadden's was structural. He was evaluating me the way he'd evaluate a load-bearing wall. Could this structure support what he needed it to support?

<<dialog "Hadden">>
You're Vane?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Aldric Vane. The archer is Mira Dunhollow.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Hadden">>
You look   young for a sellsword. Smooth-faced. I expected someone more   weathered.
<</dialog>>

[[Mira's reaction|day_5_3_3]]

:: day_5_3_3

The observation hit me like a slap. Smooth-faced. Two words that should have been a compliment and were instead an indictment. I felt Mira stiffen beside me   a subtle shift, the half-elf equivalent of bristling.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I'm twenty-six. The road ages some faster than others.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Hadden">>
Indeed. Well. The road east is approximately eighteen miles to the crossroads at Corsham, then twelve miles northeast to the site. Three days of walking, by my calculation. Two if we push.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
We don't push. Walking pace for the mule, resting when we need to, arriving alive and capable. Three days to the site. One day for your assessment. Then we part ways at the crossroads on the return leg. Agreed?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Hadden">>
Agreed. The mule's name is Pebble, and she bites. Don't try to lead her from the left side   she has a thing about the left. I don't understand it and I've stopped trying.
<</dialog>>

Pebble eyed me with the withering disdain of an animal that had indeed formed opinions about sides and was not interested in negotiating. I gave her a wide berth.

We passed through the east gate. The guards waved us through without a second glance   two armed travelers and a civilian with a mule, heading east. Unremarkable. Routine. The kind of departure that happened a dozen times a day in a city that traded in mercenary labor the way other cities traded in textiles or grain.

The Ashenmoor Road opened before us. Packed earth, wide enough for two carts abreast, running east through a landscape of low hills and scattered woodland toward the horizon. The sky was clear for the first time in days   a washed-out blue, pale as water, the sun sitting low behind us and throwing long shadows ahead. The trees that lined the road were still dripping from the recent rains, each branch hung with droplets that caught the early light and glittered like suspended jewelry.

I walked. My body walked and walking was different.

[[The Road|day_5_4]]

:: day_5_4

Not dramatically different. Not the obvious limp of the injured ankle, which had healed to the point of bare noticeability, a ghost of a hitch that appeared only on uneven ground. This was subtler. A shift in the mechanics of locomotion so incremental that a stranger wouldn't notice, that even Mira might not catch unless she was watching closely   which she always was.

My hips. The widening bones were changing the geometry of my stride. The angle at which my femurs connected to the pelvis had tilted   microscopically, a fraction of a degree   and the result was a gait that was fractionally different from the one I'd owned for twenty-six years. My legs swung from slightly wider-set hip joints, the arc of each stride marginally more lateral, the feet landing slightly farther apart. The change was invisible in trousers and boots. But I could feel it. In my hip flexors, which were working at a slightly different angle. In my inner thighs, which brushed together on each stride in a way they hadn't before, the fabric of my trousers whispering between legs that were fractionally closer together at the top. In the subtle sway that wanted to develop in my pelvis, the rotational motion that my hips were encouraging and that I was consciously suppressing, keeping my walk straight and military and male through deliberate effort.

Mira fell into step beside me. Our practiced road formation   she on the left, bow side, me on the right, sword side. The arrangement we'd refined over three months of traveling together, the spatial partnership that positioned us for optimal coverage in an ambush scenario. Her stride matched mine automatically, the way two people who'd walked hundreds of miles together synchronized without discussion.

Except today, the synchronization required adjustment. Her legs had to shorten their stride by a fraction to match mine, or mine had to lengthen to match hers, and neither of us commented on the discrepancy because commenting would make it real.

The road was well-traveled in the first few miles outside Valdmere. Merchant carts heading east toward the crossroads, loaded with goods bound for the smaller towns along the Ashenmoor corridor. A group of pilgrims in brown robes, walking in silence, heading toward a shrine that Mira said was two days south. A patrol of city guards   four men in blue-and-gray uniforms, bored and sweating despite the cool morning, their halberds resting on their shoulders like oversized walking sticks.

[[The guards|day_5_4_1]]

:: day_5_4_1

The guards glanced at us as they passed. The standard assessment   armed travelers, check. Consortium-associated civilian with surveying equipment, check. Potential threats, negative. Their eyes moved over me without pause. No double-take. No lingering examination. To them, I was a sellsword. Male. Armed. Unremarkable.

The relief I felt was disproportionate to the moment. A wave of gratitude so intense it made my jaw ache from clenching against it. They didn't see. They didn't notice. The disguise held. The tent-like tunic, the dark color, the sword at my hip, the breadth of my shoulders   all of it conspired to present a silhouette that read as male, and the guards accepted it without question.

But for how long? Tomorrow? Day After Tomorrow?, when Nella's timeline predicted that the chest growth would become visible even through clothing, when the stubble would be entirely gone, when the jawline would have softened enough that the ambiguity began?

<<dialog "Mira">>
Stop.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Stop what?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Spiraling. I can hear you spiraling. Your breathing changes when you spiral   it goes shallow and quick, like you're running inside your head even though your body is standing still.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I'm not spiraling. I'm projecting.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Projecting is spiraling with a calendar. The guards didn't notice. Nobody at the gate noticed. Hadden didn't notice anything beyond your lack of stubble, and he attributed that to youth. You pass. Today. Right now. That's what matters.
<</dialog>>

She was right. She was always right about the operational stuff, the tactical present tense, the discipline of staying in the moment rather than building elaborate anxieties about moments that hadn't arrived yet. It was one of the things that made her an excellent partner and an infuriating companion. She could compartmentalize the way other people breathed   naturally, automatically, without apparent effort.

[[Mira's compartmentalization|day_5_4_2]]

:: day_5_4_2

<<dialog "Aldric">>
How do you feel?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
What?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
About this. About walking beside me while I   while my body is  
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I feel like we have a job and a destination and a plan and walking is the implementation of all three. I feel like the weather's clear and the road is dry and Pebble hasn't tried to bite anyone yet, which is better than I expected. I feel practical.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
That's not what I asked.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
It's what I'm offering. The rest is   I'll let you know when I've sorted it. I don't serve feelings raw, Aldric. You know that. I cook them first.
<</dialog>>

She adjusted the bow on her shoulder. The leather strap creaked. She did the thing with her teeth   the arrowhead, clicking against her canines, the nervous habit she'd had since before I knew her. Click, click, click. The sound of Mira processing.

Three hours east. The road narrowed as they left the immediate vicinity of Valdmere, the merchant traffic thinning to occasional travelers. The landscape shifted   less farmland, more forest, the trees closing in on both sides of the road until the canopy filtered the morning light into a green-gold mosaic on the packed earth.

[[Hadden's pace|day_5_4_3]]

:: day_5_4_3

Hadden walked with Pebble behind us, maintaining a precise ten-pace gap that he'd established wordlessly in the first hour and maintained with surveyor's precision ever since. He'd produced a small notebook from his satchel and was making notations as he walked   observations about the road surface, the drainage patterns, the geological formations visible in the exposed rock faces along the route. He muttered to himself in a continuous, low monologue that covered structural measurements, load-bearing calculations, and occasional editorial comments about the quality of road engineering in this district.

He was, I realized, exactly the kind of client Voss had described. Competent in his domain, oblivious to everything outside it. He was evaluating the engineering of the road with the same focused intensity that I evaluated a room for threats and exits. Different domains. Same level of absorption. He wasn't going to notice my jawline because he didn't notice jawlines. He noticed stress fractures, drainage angles, and mortar composition. My face was outside his professional scope. Small mercies.

Mira fell into step closer beside me. Her shoulder brushed mine as the road narrowed through a stretch of overhanging trees, and even through the tunic, the contact registered with amplified clarity   the specific pressure of her shoulder against my upper arm, the warmth of her body through layers of fabric.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Give me your hand.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
What?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Your hand. I want to check something.
<</dialog>>

I extended my right hand. She took it without breaking stride, turned it palm-up, and ran her thumb across the pad below my fingers. The callus ridge. The swordsman's mark. Her thumb pressed into the diminishing ridge of hardened skin and I watched her brow furrow.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Softer. Noticeably softer than yesterday. The edges are thinning.
<</dialog>>

She turned my hand over. Examined the back   the skin smoother, the hair sparser, the veins more visible through the lighter, finer texture.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Your hands are   getting elegant. I don't know another word for it. The fingers are the same length but they look longer because they're slimmer. The knuckles are less prominent. The nails are   cleaner. Not just clean. The nail beds are smoother. There's a sheen to them.
<</dialog>>

She pressed my hand flat against hers, palm to palm. Her hand was rough. Calloused. The archer's calluses on her fingertips and the pad below her fingers were thick and hard, earned through years of drawing a bowstring hundreds of times a day. My hand against hers was   the softer hand. The smoother hand. The hand that would lose the handshake comparison that any two mercenaries would perform as a measure of shared roughness.

She laced her fingers through mine. Squeezed once. Her calluses scraped against my thinning ones and the difference in texture was a sentence written in skin.

<<dialog "Mira">>
We'll get you leather wraps for the sword grip. Tonight, at camp, I'll cut strips from the spare leather in the supply bag. Your grip strength is fine   it's just the skin that's changing. The wraps will compensate.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Compensate.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Adapt. Adjust. Whatever word doesn't make you spiral. The point i andyou can still hold a sword. The how is changing. The can doesn't.
<</dialog>>

She released my hand. We kept walking. The road stretched east, the sun climbing behind us, and every step carried me further from Valdmere and further into a body that was being remade by an alchemist's formula and a witch's intentions and the relentless, patient chemistry of thirty days I hadn't asked for.

[[Midday Rest|day_5_5]]

:: day_5_5

We stopped at a waypoint around the fifth hour   a cleared area beside the road with a stone fire ring blackened by generations of travelers' cooking fires and a wooden bench that had been carved from a single log and had sagged in the middle until it resembled a tired smile. A creek ran nearby   I could hear it, the chatter of water over stones, close but screened by undergrowth.

Hadden sat on the bench with his legs precisely together and his satchel on his knees. He produced bread and hard cheese from a wrapped parcel and began eating with the mechanical efficiency of a man who treated nutrition as a system requirement rather than a sensory experience. Each bite was the same size. Each chew was the same duration. He ate the way he walked   measured, precise, producing consistent results without joy or complaint.

Mira built a small fire in the ring and boiled water for tea. She'd brought dried mint leaves from the market   one of her purchases I hadn't tracked in the blur of yesterday's shopping expedition. The mint was strong and sharp and when I drank it, the taste registered with the new, amplified clarity that was becoming familiar. Every note of the flavor profile was distinct   the bright menthol top note, the slightly bitter undertone, the warmth of the water, the specific mineral quality of stream water that Mira had collected and boiled.

I ate jerky and hard biscuit. The jerky was salt-cured and the salt hit my tongue like a shout   vivid, overwhelming, the sodium receptors on my taste buds firing at a volume that made my eyes water. I'd eaten this same jerky yesterday and tasted "salt" and "meat." Today I tasted the specific cure   the blend of salt and pepper and something smoky, the age of the meat, the particular chewiness of muscle fiber that had been dried to preservation but retained the ghost of its living texture.

My body was becoming an instrument of higher resolution. Every input   taste, touch, smell, temperature   was being received with greater fidelity, the sensory bandwidth expanded by whatever the compound was doing to my nervous system. The world was louder. More detailed. More present and I couldn't turn it down.

Hadden finished his bread and cheese, wiped his fingers on a handkerchief   an actual linen handkerchief, monogrammed, the kind of personal accessory that screamed "I have never slept outdoors voluntarily in my life"   and returned his attention to his notebook.

[[Hadden's observations|day_5_5_1]]

:: day_5_5_1

<<dialog "Hadden">>
The road surface has deteriorated significantly past the five-mile marker. Water damage from the recent rains. The drainage infrastructure is wholly inadequate for the volume. I've noted six points where the sub-road foundation has eroded to critical levels. If the Consortium wants to maintain this route as a primary trade corridor, they'll need to invest in proper culvert systems.
<</dialog>>

He said this to his notebook, to the fire, to the trees. Not to us. But Mira responded anyway, because Mira treated silence as a vacuum that conversation should fill.

<<dialog "Mira">>
How long have you been doing this? Surveying sites for the Consortium?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Hadden">>
Twelve years. Before that, I was a structural assessor for the Crown's Building Commission in the capital. Before that, I studied at the Academy of Engineering in Whitestone. I have evaluated approximately three hundred and forty sites across seven provinces and my reports have a less than two percent error rate on structural integrity predictions.
<</dialog>>

He said this without pride. It was data. A personal specification sheet delivered with the same flat precision he applied to road surfaces.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Impressive. The site we're heading to   the old estate storehouse. What do you expect to find?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Hadden">>
Collapsed upper structure, likely timber frame over stone foundation. The earthquake sixty years ago would have sheared the frame from the foundation at the junction points   that's where earthquake damage concentrates, at the interfaces between rigid and flexible structural elements. The stone cellar, if properly vaulted, may be intact. Sealed cargo in a stone vault can survive indefinitely if the vault maintained its integrity.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
The cargo is alchemical reagents?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Hadden">>
Among other things. The estate belonged to a merchant family that operated a private trade route for specialty goods   alchemical compounds, rare herbs, prepared ingredients. The earthquake destroyed the estate and the family relocated. The cellar was never excavated. The Consortium acquired the land rights last year and wants to know what's recoverable.
<</dialog>>

[[Alchemical compounds|day_5_5_2]]

:: day_5_5_2

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Alchemical compounds. Do you know what kind?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Hadden">>
I don't concern myself with contents until I've assessed the container. Contents are a different department's problem. My job is to tell the Consortium whether the structure can be safely entered and whether the cargo containers are intact. What's inside those containers is above my professional interest and, frankly, below my expertise.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
But you've encountered alchemical materials before? In other surveys?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Hadden">>
Three times. Twice the containers were compromised and the reagents had degraded to inert compounds. Once   once the containers were intact and the contents were still active. That was the Bellmoore cache, four years ago. Sealed glass vials in lead-lined wooden crates. Sixty years old and the compounds were not only viable but more potent than fresh equivalents. The Consortium sold them for extraordinary sums.
<</dialog>>

He pushed his spectacles up his nose with one precise finger. The gesture was mechanical, habitual, the kind of motion that had been performed so many times it had worn a faint groove on the bridge of his nose.

<<dialog "Hadden">>
Alchemists are an eccentric profession. They create things that defy normal material science   compounds that improve with age, liquids that seem to have their own volition, substances that interact with biological systems in ways that proper chemistry considers impossible. I'm an engineer. I deal with forces and materials that obey consistent laws. Alchemy is   unsettling. It doesn't follow rules.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
No. It doesn't.
<</dialog>>

Mira caught my eye. The look she gave me was carefully calibrated   don't push it. Don't ask more. Don't draw attention to why a sellsword would have opinions about the rule-following behavior of alchemical compounds.

I took the hint. Finished my jerky. Stood, stretching, my body protesting with the particular stiffness that came from sitting on a stone fire ring with an aching pelvis and a transforming spine. I needed to move and I needed to check.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Going to relieve myself. Back in a minute.
<</dialog>>

Nobody looked up. The universal social contract of outdoor travel   when someone announces the need for a bush, you don't watch them find one.

[[Private check|day_5_5_3]]

:: day_5_5_3

I went behind the screen of scrub brush that separated the waypoint from the creek. The sound of water covered any sounds I might make. The undergrowth was thick enough to provide visual privacy in every direction.

I unlaced my trousers. Pushed them down past my hips   the hips that required more clearance than they used to, the fabric dragging against bone that sat wider than the trouser's original cut had anticipated. I pushed the smallclothes down.

/* <img src="img/scene/day_5/5.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" /> */
Standing in the dappled forest light, naked from the waist down, I performed the inventory that was becoming ritual. The daily check. The measurement of loss. The accounting of a body being audited by forces beyond its owner's control.

My cock. I looked at it first because looking at it first was an act of defiance, a prioritization that insisted the most male part of me be confirmed before anything else was assessed. It hung between my thighs. Present. Unchanged in size, shape, weight. The foreskin rested in its natural position, the shaft thick and familiar against thighs that were   I looked more carefully.

The thighs. The inner thighs. The skin there was softer than it had been   noticeably, dramatically softer. Where rough, hair-textured skin had been, there was now a surface that approached silk. The body hair on my inner thighs had thinned to fine, nearly invisible fuzz, and the skin beneath was smooth and responsive in a way that made my breath catch when my fingers brushed across it. The nerve density in this region had been amplified. Dramatically. My fingertips against my own inner thigh sent a cascade of sensation upward   not quite sexual, not quite neutral, occupying a charged middle ground that made my cock twitch in sympathetic response.

I cupped myself. The familiar gesture. Testicles present and accounted for, hanging in a scrotum that was   was it smoother? The rough, textured skin of the scrotum seemed marginally finer, the wrinkles less deep, the overall surface approaching a smoothness that scrotal skin was not supposed to achieve. Or was I imagining it? Projecting changes that hadn't happened yet because Nella's timeline had told me they were coming?

[[The perineum|day_5_5_4]]

:: day_5_5_4

I pressed two fingers against my perineum. The strip of skin between the scrotum and the anus. Here. This was where Nella's careful, clinical description had told me the most dramatic changes would eventually manifest. Where the tissue would reshape. Where the anatomy would be restructured. Where the compound would perform its most radical act of revision.

The skin was the same as it had always been. Warm. Smooth-ish. Unremarkable. No changes. No tenderness. No indication that this particular territory was scheduled for demolition and reconstruction.

Not yet, Nella had said. Sooner genital changes to begin. Six days from now. Six days until the compound turned its attention to the one piece of my anatomy that I could not bear to lose.

I let go. Pulled my smallclothes up, trousers up, laced them, buckled the belt at the new, tighter notch.

The creek was right there. I could hear it, see it through the last curtain of brush   clear water running over smooth stones, maybe a foot deep, catching the morning light in flashes of silver. I knelt at the bank and washed my hands. The water was cold   mountain-fed, autumn-chilled   and when it hit my skin, the sensation was extraordinary. Every nerve ending in my hands fired simultaneously, the cold registering not as a single temperature but as a complex, multi-layered experience. The pressure of the current against my palms. The texture of the water itself   its weight, its resistance, its specific liquid quality. The individual stones beneath my fingers, each one a different shape, a different smoothness, a different temperature.

I splashed water on my face. The cold hit my cheeks, my jaw, the smooth skin where stubble should have been, and the sensation was so vivid that I gasped. Not from cold. From the sheer volume of information that my face was suddenly receiving. The water ran down my neck, into the collar of the tunic, and each droplet was a discrete event, a separate point of contact with a specific trajectory and a specific temperature and a specific weight.

My skin was becoming a sense organ. Not just the passive boundary between inside and outside that it had always been, but an active, aggressive receiver of data. The world was pressing against me with an intimacy that was overwhelming. Every breeze was a touch. Every fabric was a conversation. Every temperature change was a story told in nerve impulses that my brain was learning to read in a new language.

I dried my face on my sleeve. Stood. Went back to the waypoint.

Mira handed me a cup of mint tea without looking at me. She'd poured it while I was away, and it was the perfect temperature   warm enough to taste, cool enough to drink. The kind of small, unremarked-upon care that she performed constantly and acknowledged never.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Everything where it should be?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
For now.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Good. Drink your tea. We move in ten minutes.
<</dialog>>

I drank the tea. The mint was vivid on my tongue. The warmth spread through my chest, through the tissue behind the nipples, through the narrowing waist, through the aching hips. Through the body that was mine and not mine and changing between one heartbeat and the next.

[[The Afternoon Road|day_5_6]]

:: day_5_6

The afternoon stretched. The road unwound east through increasingly wild terrain   the scattered woodlands of Valdmere's outskirts giving way to denser forest, the canopy thickening overhead until the sunlight reached the road only in patches, golden coins scattered on packed earth. The air was cooler under the trees. Damper. Rich with the smell of leaf decay and wet bark and the particular fungal musk of a forest floor that hadn't dried out from the recent rains.

We walked in the formation that three months of partnership had refined into instinct. Mira on the left, eyes scanning the tree line, bow accessible, her body occupying the low-level alertness that was her default state on open roads. Me on the right, sword at my hip, my own awareness divided between the potential threats in the forest and the confirmed threat inside my own body.

Hadden walked behind us with Pebble, maintaining his precise ten-pace gap. The mule's hooves beat a steady rhythm on the packed earth   clop, clop, clop, clop   a metronome set to the tempo of cantankerous animal. Hadden's muttered observations had shifted from road engineering to geological analysis of the exposed rock faces along the route. He was, I was beginning to realize, a man who perceived the world exclusively through the lens of structural systems. Rock was not rock to him   it was a matrix of minerals under specific pressures, a data set describing forces and failures and the long, slow mechanics of geological time.

I envied him. His body was not a structural system under forced renovation. His body was just a vehicle that carried his brain to the next interesting rock formation.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Marsh. The ruins site   you mentioned the Consortium believes it may contain vintage alchemical reagents. If they found such reagents   compounds that had been sealed for sixty years   what would they do with them?
<</dialog>>

Hadden looked up from his notebook with the expression of a man being asked about his area of expertise by someone outside his professional circle. A blend of mild surprise and the particular patience of a specialist condescending to explain fundamentals.

<<dialog "Hadden">>
Sell them. Obviously. Vintage reagents are valued by alchemists the way vintage wines are valued by merchants   aged compounds develop complexity, potency, and in some cases entirely new properties that fresh preparations lack. A sixty-year-old sealed vial of properly preserved feminization compound, for example, would be worth  
<</dialog>>

[[Stop walking|day_5_6_1]]

:: day_5_6_1

I stopped walking.

<<dialog "Hadden">>
   ten times its fresh equivalent, because the catalytic agents have had decades to fully integrate with the base solution, creating a compound of significantly higher efficacy and  
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
What did you say?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Hadden">>
   higher efficacy? It's basic alchemical aging theory. Time allows molecular bonds to  
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
No. Before that. You said feminization compound. Specifically.
<</dialog>>

Hadden blinked. Pushed his spectacles up his nose.

<<dialog "Hadden">>
Did I? It was illustrative. I could have said any compound   healing draught, sleep elixir, strength tonic. Feminization agents simply came to mind because they're among the most valuable vintage reagents. A fresh preparation sells for fifteen gold. A sixty-year-old preparation, properly sealed? Fifty gold. Perhaps more. The aging process makes the transformation faster, more thorough, and   according to the alchemists who work with them   more elegant in its results.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
You know a surprising amount about feminization compounds for a structural surveyor.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Hadden">>
I know a surprising amount about many things. It's a professional hazard. When you catalog sites that contain alchemical cargo, you develop a working knowledge of what that cargo is and what it's worth. The Consortium briefing materials are thorough, even if most surveyors don't read them and I read everything.
<</dialog>>

[[Continue walking|day_5_6_2]]

:: day_5_6_2

He returned to his notebook. The moment had passed. For him, it had been an unremarkable tangent   an illustrative example, no more significant than citing the aging properties of wine or cheese. He didn't know what he'd said. He didn't know what the word "feminization" did to the man walking ten paces ahead of him, the man whose body was currently demonstrating exactly the properties he'd described   transformation that was fast, thorough, and   the word landed like a blade between my ribs   elegant.

Mira's hand found my elbow. A brief squeeze. Grounding. Her fingers on the inside of my arm, pressing through the linen into skin that received the contact with amplified, treacherous sensitivity.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Coincidence. He doesn't know.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
He said the aging process makes it   more elegant.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
He was talking about sixty-year-old compounds in sealed vials. Not about you. He doesn't know.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
But what if the compound Ysolde gave me   what if it was aged? What if that's why it's working faster than Nella expected?
<</dialog>>

Mira was quiet for several strides. I could hear her processing   the arrowhead click of her teeth, the particular rhythm of her breathing when she was running calculations that she didn't like the results of.

<<dialog "Mira">>
We don't know that. We don't know anything about the compound's age or provenance. What we know is that Nella said you were running ahead of schedule. That could be the compound's age. It could be your individual physiology. It could be the conditions under which it was administered   you drank it with a wound infection, your body was already in a heightened metabolic state. There are multiple explanations.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
All of which end in the same place.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
All of which end in us finding Ysolde and getting the anti-compound. The speed of the process doesn't change the solution. It just changes the urgency. Walk.
<</dialog>>

I walked. The road continued east. The sun continued its arc overhead. The mule continued her rhythmic, contemptuous advance behind us and inside me, the compound continued its work   patient, elegant, indifferent to my opinion on the beauty of its results.

[[Travelers on the road|day_5_7]]

:: day_5_7

Mid-afternoon. The road widened briefly where a side track branched south toward a hamlet, and in the widened section, we encountered other travelers for the first time since the morning patrols.

A family. Father, mother, two children   a boy of maybe eight and a girl of maybe five, riding in a small cart pulled by a horse that made Pebble look like an optimist. They were heading west, toward Valdmere, the cart loaded with produce   bushels of late-season apples, sacks of root vegetables, the agricultural output of a small farm being brought to market. The father drove. The mother sat beside him. The children sat in the back among the apple bushels, the boy reading a book and the girl methodically eating an apple with the focused dedication of someone conducting important research.

The father raised a hand as we approached   the universal greeting of road travelers, the open palm that meant I come in peace, or at least I come without a weapon in this particular hand. I returned the gesture. Mira nodded. Hadden didn't look up from his notebook.

We passed. Close enough that the father's cart horse and Pebble had to negotiate right-of-way, which they did with the mutual hostility of animals that had formed opinions about each other on sight. Close enough that the mother got a good look at our group   the armed pair, the civilian with the mule, the equipment, the supplies.

Close enough that the mother looked at me. Directly. A long, evaluating look that started at my face and moved down and came back to my face and stayed there.

<<dialog "Mother">>
Good day. You're heading east?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Good day. East to the crossroads.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mother">>
Road's clear for the next ten miles. We passed a merchant caravan this morning   eight wagons, well-guarded. No trouble to report. Travel safe.
<</dialog>>

She smiled. A pleasant, farm-wife smile, the expression of a woman who'd raised children and managed a household and developed the ability to categorize strangers quickly and without malice. But behind the smile, something lingered. A question she hadn't asked. A category she hadn't quite settled me into.

[[Cart passes|day_5_7_1]]

:: day_5_7_1

The cart passed. The apple-eating girl waved at me from among the bushels. I waved back. My hand   my smoother, softer, increasingly elegant hand   rose and fell in the wave, and the girl's eyes tracked it with the unfiltered curiosity of a child who noticed everything and judged nothing.

They were gone. The road was ours again.

<<dialog "Mira">>
She looked at you.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I noticed.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Not suspiciously. Just   she was reading you. Filing you. You registered as something she needed an extra second to categorize. She categorized you as male   I saw her settle on it. But the extra second was there. The question was there.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
It's the face. The jawline. The lips.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
It's the skin. The face is still reading male   barely, but it's reading. The skin, though. Your skin is too smooth. Too clear. Too   luminous. You look like a young lord, not a sellsword. Sellswords don't have skin like that. That's what caught her. The mismatch between the sword and the skin.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
So I need to look dirtier.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
You need to look more weather-beaten. Roll in some mud tonight. Get dust on your face during the walk. Keep the tunic collar high and the gloves   once we make camp, I'll rig something from leather scraps. Gloves hide the hands. The hands are the second biggest tell after the face.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
The second biggest?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
For now. In a few days, the biggest tell will be the chest and we'll have the wrap for that. One day at a time, Aldric. One adjustment at a time.
<</dialog>>

One adjustment at a time. One compromise at a time. One piece of the disguise assembled each day to compensate for what the compound was disassembling inside me, building a costume of masculine presentation over a body that was shedding its masculinity the way a snake sheds skin   slowly, continuously, the old surface sloughing away to reveal something newer and softer and entirely unsolicited beneath.

[[Evening Camp|day_5_8]]

:: day_5_8

We made camp as the sun began its descent, the western sky going amber and rose above the tree line. The site was good   a natural clearing beside the road, screened by dense undergrowth on three sides and bounded on the fourth by a creek that was wider and deeper than the one at the midday waypoint. The water ran clear over a bed of smooth stones, maybe two feet deep at the center, the surface catching the sunset light in a rippling pattern of gold and shadow.

Mira selected the site. Mira always selected the site. She evaluated terrain the way Hadden evaluated structures   sightlines, escape routes, defensible positions, proximity to water. This clearing met her criteri andvisual cover from the road, water access, flat ground for bedrolls, and a natural choke point at the gap between the undergrowth and the creek where anyone approaching would have to funnel into a two-body-width opening that one archer could hold indefinitely.

Hadden set up his bedroll with the fussy precision of a man who had learned to camp from a manual rather than from experience. Each element was positioned according to some internal specification   the bedroll exactly parallel to the fire ring, the satchel at a forty-five-degree angle, the notebook and pen within reach of his right hand. Pebble was tethered to a tree, where she immediately began eating the tree's bark with the vindictive enthusiasm of an animal that had decided, if she was going to be tied to something, she was going to destroy it.

I gathered firewood. The physical work was welcome   the bending, the lifting, the breaking of branches across my knee. My body still worked for these tasks. The arms were still strong. The legs were still powerful. The muscle mass hadn't changed   not yet, not visibly. The compound was rewriting the surface while the structure beneath remained, for now, the body of a working fighter.

But the surface changes colored the work. My hands, gathering wood, were softer against the rough bark. The broken ends of branches scratched skin that flinched at the texture, the sensitivity amplifying what should have been background noise into foreground signal. A splinter drove under the skin of my thumb and the pain was   more. Sharper. A bright, precise hurt that radiated from the point of entry with a specificity that my old, calloused hands would have barely registered.

I sucked the splinter out. The taste of my own blood was   different? The same? I couldn't tell anymore. Everything tasted different. Everything felt different. The compound had rewired my input channels without providing an instruction manual for the new settings.

Mira built the fire with her usual efficiency   a pyramid of tinder and kindling that caught on the first strike of her flint, blooming into flame with the satisfying whoosh of air being consumed. The firelight pushed back the gathering dusk and painted our clearing in warm amber, the shadows beyond the fire's reach deepening to blue-black.

Hadden retreated to his bedroll and his notebook. Within twenty minutes, his pen had stopped moving and his head had tilted forward and his breathing had shifted to the slow, rhythmic pattern of unconsciousness. He slept the way he lived   precisely, efficiently, with zero tolerance for deviation from schedule.

[[Sword maintenance|day_5_8_1]]

:: day_5_8_1

<<dialog "Mira">>
Out like a candle. Good. He'll sleep through anything.
<</dialog>>

